// ---------------- abstract ----------------
children.push(h1("Abstract"));
children.push(P("Automated verification of ICAO/ISO compliance for face images in machine-readable travel documents is typically performed by rule-based analyzers that return hard pass/fail decisions per requirement. Such decisions carry no statistical guarantee, and their false rejections translate into unnecessary photo retakes, while a missed violation is far more costly, so the operational problem is triage under class imbalance and asymmetric error cost. We wrap an existing rule-based analyzer (BioGaze) with a lightweight per-criterion supervised meta-classifier followed by conformal calibration, and study, on the TONO synthetic ICAO benchmark under a strictly subject-disjoint protocol, what distribution-free guarantees a multi-criterion compliance pipeline can and cannot deliver. Our study is empirical and diagnostic rather than a new estimator. We report four findings. First, marginal split-conformal holds its overall coverage target (0.90) but silently under-covers the minority violation class on 5 of the 14 criteria (6 if a small-sample criterion is counted), meaning that the whole 95% interval lies below the 0.90 target (as low as 0.40 with an interval of 0.34 to 0.46), and label-conditional (Mondrian) conformal brings per-class coverage close to the nominal 0.90 target on every stable criterion, consistent with label-conditional validity; primary intervals come from a subject-cluster bootstrap on one fixed partition, with ten repeated partitions as a robustness analysis. Second, through a feature ablation we show the coverage property is robust to feature quality within the evaluated distribution, as theory predicts, whereas most of the apparent false-flag reduction is an artifact of the single-violation dataset structure and is not, by itself, evidence for conformal prediction; validity on real multi-attribute data remains to be tested. Third, across seven triage strategies, including two abstention rules tuned on calibration data only and compared at a matched abstention rate, no method dominates: recalibration supplies most of the accuracy, a conventional abstention rule reaches a lower false-positive rate than Mondrian at the same retake rate, and Mondrian's distinct contribution is the formal class-conditional coverage property: its two thresholds are fixed as calibration quantiles at the nominal level rather than searched against a cost objective. A correctly randomized adaptive prediction set (APS) is competitive rather than degenerate. Fourth, a 14-criterion image-level decision incurs a multiple-comparison problem: naive joint coverage collapses to about 0.27, and a Bonferroni-corrected image-level guarantee of about 0.91 is attainable only by routing almost every image to retake. We confine claims to what TONO can support: it is single-violation, so the ACCEPT class is empty and cannot be validated, which motivates real multi-attribute data as follow-up."));

// ---------------- 1 introduction ----------------
children.push(h1("1. Introduction"));
children.push(P("Face images in passports and other machine-readable travel documents must satisfy the photographic requirements of ICAO Doc 9303 and ISO/IEC 39794-5: a frontal pose and neutral expression, open eyes, uniform lighting and background, no sunglasses or head covering, and adequate sharpness, exposure and colour fidelity, among others. At enrolment these requirements are increasingly checked by automated analyzers rather than by trained officers alone. Practical analyzers, including the open-source BioGaze framework we build on, decompose the standard into per-requirement checks and return a hard pass or fail verdict for each. Such verdicts carry no statistical guarantee, and their errors have asymmetric costs. A false flag sends a compliant applicant back for an unnecessary retake, an operational nuisance at scale; a missed violation lets a non-compliant photo into a document, where it can degrade every downstream use of the image, which is far more costly. True violations are also the minority class in a realistic enrolment stream. The operational problem is therefore not plain classification accuracy but uncertainty-aware triage under class imbalance and asymmetric error cost: decide ACCEPT or REJECT when the evidence is clear, and route residual uncertainty to an explicit RETAKE."));
children.push(P("This paper asks whether the outputs of such an existing rule-based analyzer can be endowed with a distribution-free statistical guarantee, and turned into that triage, without modifying or retraining the analyzer. The natural candidate is conformal prediction, which converts any predictor's scores into prediction sets with a user-chosen coverage level, at the price of an exchangeability assumption rather than a model assumption. The subtlety is which guarantee one buys. The standard marginal formulation guarantees coverage on average over both classes, and under class imbalance it can hold exactly while failing silently on the minority class, which here is the class whose errors are costly. The guarantee that matters for compliance triage is class-conditional, and the label-conditional (Mondrian) formulation provides it by calibrating within each class."));
children.push(P("We wrap each of BioGaze's 14 per-criterion decisions with a lightweight supervised meta-classifier followed by split conformal calibration, and evaluate marginal LAC, Mondrian and randomized adaptive prediction sets on the TONO synthetic ICAO benchmark under a strictly subject-disjoint protocol, since the same synthetic identity recurs across violation types and an image-level split would leak identities between calibration and test. Because an abstaining method can look good on false positives simply by abstaining often, we compare against deliberately strong conventional baselines, Chow's confidence-rejection rule and an asymmetric two-threshold rule tuned on calibration data only, at matched abstention rates. We treat the contribution as empirical and diagnostic. That marginal conformal can under-cover a minority class, and that label-conditional calibration addresses it, are known properties; our value is in stress-testing them on a structured, 14-decision compliance pipeline, quantifying what each method does and does not deliver, and reporting honestly which observed benefits are artifacts of the evaluation data."));
children.push(P("Four findings summarise the study. First, on 5 of the 14 criteria marginal conformal under-covers the violation class badly enough that the entire interval lies below the target, while Mondrian brings violation-class coverage close to the nominal target on every stable criterion, consistent with label-conditional validity. Second, a feature ablation shows that the coverage property is robust to feature quality within the evaluated distribution, whereas the apparent accuracy rides a single-violation dataset artifact; its validity on real multi-attribute data remains to be tested. Third, across seven triage strategies no method dominates: a calibration-tuned abstention rule reaches an equal or better empirical operating point than any conformal method, and what Mondrian adds is the formal class-conditional coverage property, whose level is set in advance rather than tuned. Fourth, a 14-criterion image-level decision faces a multiple-comparison problem: naive joint coverage collapses to about 0.27, and the Bonferroni repair is attainable but operationally vacuous at this calibration size."));
children.push(new Paragraph({ children: [new TextRun({ text: "Contributions.", bold: true })], spacing: { after: 60 } }));
children.push(bullet("A model-agnostic per-criterion supervised meta-classifier with conformal calibration that endows each of 14 ICAO decisions of a rule-based analyzer with a distribution-free coverage guarantee, evaluated under a subject-disjoint protocol with confidence intervals over ten splits."));
children.push(bullet("Evidence, with subject-cluster bootstrap intervals on a fixed primary partition and a 10-partition robustness analysis, that marginal conformal under-covers the minority violation class and that label-conditional (Mondrian) conformal brings its coverage close to the nominal target, the cost-relevant quantity."));
children.push(bullet("A feature ablation separating the coverage property, which is robust to feature quality within the evaluated distribution, from the accuracy, which rides a single-violation dataset artifact, and an honest seven-method trade-off with calibration-only tuning and a matched-abstention comparison, in which recalibration provides most of the accuracy, a conventional abstention rule provides an equal or better empirical operating point, and Mondrian provides the formal class-conditional coverage property."));
children.push(bullet("An image-level triage analysis that decomposes rejections into correct-target versus wrong-reason, a characterisation of the multiple-comparison problem intrinsic to a 14-criterion decision (a Bonferroni-corrected joint guarantee and its conservatism cost), and an explicit statement of what TONO cannot evaluate."));

// ---------------- 2 related work ----------------
children.push(P("The paper is organised as follows. Section 2 reviews conformal prediction, selective classification and automated ICAO compliance. Section 3 describes the pipeline, the subject-disjoint protocol and the seven triage strategies. Section 4 presents the results: the feature ablation, per-criterion coverage, the trade-off study, image-level triage, joint coverage, robustness, and the exchangeability diagnostics. Section 5 illustrates region-level explanations, Section 6 discusses operational implications, external validity and limitations, and Section 7 concludes."));
children.push(h1("2. Related work"));
children.push(P2("Conformal prediction and class-conditional validity. ", "Conformal prediction provides distribution-free, finite-sample coverage for any base predictor under exchangeability (Vovk, Gammerman and Shafer; Angelopoulos and Bates). We use the least-ambiguous-set (LAC) non-conformity score of Sadinle, Lei and Wasserman, and compare against the adaptive prediction sets (APS) of Romano, Sesia and Candes in their correctly randomized form. Coverage on a minority class is a known weakness of the marginal formulation: the guarantee averages over the label distribution, so a scarce class can be under-covered while overall coverage is exact. The label-conditional (Mondrian) construction of Vovk calibrates within each class and guarantees coverage per label, and Ding et al. study the class-conditional problem when classes are many and small. Our per-criterion problem is the binary instance of this idea, replicated across 14 coupled criteria, which raises a joint-coverage (multiple-comparison) question that single-decision studies do not face. Conformal risk control (Angelopoulos et al.; Bates et al.) generalises coverage to other risk functionals; we use it only to reason about operating points."));
children.push(P2("Selective classification and the reject option. ", "Abstaining when uncertain goes back to Chow, who characterised the optimal reject rule under known posteriors; El-Yaniv and Wiener formalised the risk-coverage trade-off, and Geifman and El-Yaniv integrated the reject option into deep models. Our RETAKE outcome is exactly a reject option, and our two conventional baselines instantiate this line: Chow's confidence-rejection rule, and an asymmetric two-threshold band tuned against a miss-weighted cost. Conformal prediction sets induce the same structure implicitly, with ambiguous or empty sets acting as rejections, which is why we compare all methods at matched abstention rates rather than on raw accuracy. Our finding that a well-tuned conventional rule matches or beats the conformal methods empirically, while only the conformal construction carries a finite-sample validity property, mirrors the general picture in selective classification."));
children.push(P2("Automated ICAO/ISO compliance and face image quality. ", "Automated compliance verification has been studied primarily as point classification. Ferrara, Franco and Maltoni established conformance checking and the BioLab-ICAO benchmark; face-image-quality assessment more broadly (the surveys of Schlett et al. and Terhorst et al., and the open-source OFIQ reference implementation of ISO/IEC 29794-5) produces scalar quality scores or per-requirement verdicts. The most recent line is the BioGaze framework of Elatfi et al., which combines classical vision and deep models to check the photographic requirements of ISO/IEC 39794-5 and is the analyzer we wrap; its companion synthetic datasets ONOT and TONO (Di Domenico et al.; Borghi et al.); a zero-shot formulation through CLIP-IQA and natural-language prompting (Di Domenico et al., 2025); and the DFIC dataset of Goncalves et al. (2026), a real, demographically balanced collection of about 58,000 images annotated for 26 requirements, on which BioGaze, OFIQ and ICAONet are compared and generalisation to TONO is measured."));
children.push(P2("Positioning. ", "All of the systems above return hard per-requirement verdicts or scalar scores; none attaches a distribution-free coverage guarantee to its decisions, none distinguishes a confident rejection from a case that should be retaken, and none reports coverage on the violation class, which is the gap we address. Because our layer is model-agnostic and consumes only the analyzer's outputs, it applies unchanged to any of these systems, and DFIC, with its genuine compliant class, is the natural target for the external validation discussed in Section 6."));

// ---------------- 3 method ----------------
children.push(h1("3. Method"));
children.push(P("Figure 1 summarises the pipeline: BioGaze produces per-criterion decisions and continuous metrics, a per-criterion meta-classifier maps them to a violation probability, split conformal prediction turns that probability into a prediction set with a coverage guarantee, and the 14 sets are aggregated into an accept, reject or retake decision."));
children.push(...figure(R1 + "pipeline.png", 560, 214, "Figure 1. Model-agnostic conformal layer on a rule-based ICAO analyzer: BioGaze features, per-criterion classifier, split-conformal prediction sets, and an accept/reject/retake decision."));
children.push(h2("3.1 Features and per-criterion meta-classifier"));
children.push(P("For each image, BioGaze produces 14 binary ICAO decisions together with continuous metrics (inter-eye distance, head-pose angles, eye and mouth openness, and a glasses indicator). For each criterion we fit a class-balanced logistic regression to predict the ground-truth label, violation versus compliant, from these outputs. This is a supervised meta-classifier over all BioGaze-derived features, not a probability calibration of a single criterion's own decision, followed by conformal calibration of its scores; the analyzer itself is never retrained. Section 4.1 examines, by ablation, which features actually drive the result, since using all 14 decisions lets the classifier exploit cross-criterion structure specific to the single-violation dataset."));
children.push(h2("3.2 Subject-disjoint evaluation"));
children.push(P("The TONO subject identifier is parsed from each filename. Of 275 identities, 247 appear in more than one violation folder, so a split stratified by violation type would place the same identity in calibration and test and inflate coverage. We therefore partition identities, not images, at 60/20/20 into train, calibration and test, with zero identity overlap (verified). Every headline number is reported as a summary over multiple subject-disjoint splits (five for aggregate comparisons, ten for per-criterion coverage). The primary statistical analysis is conducted on one fixed partition (partition 1). Per-criterion coverage is reported with 95% intervals from a cluster bootstrap that resamples the 55 test subjects with replacement, keeping each subject's images together, so that within-subject dependence is respected by construction. The repeated partitions serve as a robustness analysis, reported as mean, SD and range over ten partitions (Section 4.2); aggregate method comparisons use five partitions with mean and SD (Section 4.3). Because a subject contributes several correlated images (median 14 to 15 per subject in every split), image-level exchangeability of calibration and test scores is an assumption of the analysis rather than a consequence of the split; we state the guarantee at the image level under that assumption and test its sensitivity to within-subject dependence in Section 4.7."));
children.push(P("Scope of the label space. TONO's zoom folder depicts a head that is too large for the ICAO framing envelope, a head-size criterion that BioGaze does not check. These images carry no in-scope label, so they are excluded from every per-criterion coverage and trade-off computation (Sections 4.1 to 4.3 and 4.5 to 4.7) and appear only in the image-level counts of Section 4.4, where they are reported separately as out-of-scope. Every in-scope image violates exactly one criterion by construction."));
children.push(h2("3.3 Split conformal: marginal, Mondrian, and APS"));
children.push(P("We apply split conformal prediction with the LAC score s = 1 minus the predicted probability of the true class. On the calibration split we take the exact finite-sample threshold as the k-th order statistic with k = ceil((n+1)(1 minus alpha)), returning the trivial include-all set when calibration is too small to certify. A class is included in an image's set when 1 minus its predicted probability is at most the threshold. The marginal formulation uses one threshold; the label-conditional (Mondrian) formulation estimates a separate threshold per class, which enforces coverage within the minority violation class. We also evaluate a correctly randomized APS. The layer is implemented from scratch for full control and cross-checked against the MAPIE library for the marginal case (agreement within 0.003 coverage)."));
children.push(h2("3.4 Triage strategies and baselines"));
children.push(P("On the same five subject-disjoint partitions we compare seven strategies: the raw BioGaze rule, the logistic classifier alone, two conventional abstention rules, marginal LAC, Mondrian, and randomized APS. The abstention rules are chosen as strong rather than weak baselines. The first is Chow's confidence-rejection rule: abstain when max(p, 1 minus p) is below a confidence level tau, otherwise decide by the majority class. The second is an asymmetric two-threshold rule that decides compliant when p is at most t_lo, decides violation when p is at least t_hi, and abstains in between, with the pair chosen to minimise a cost of ten per missed violation plus one per false flag among decided cases. Every tuned quantity is set on the calibration split only: tau is chosen so that the calibration abstention rate is closest to a fixed design target of 0.10, and (t_lo, t_hi) is chosen with calibration abstention within 0.02 of the same target. The target of 0.10 is a pre-specified operational comparison point, chosen because it is close to the RETAKE rate that Mondrian produces empirically at the 0.90 level, so that the methods can be compared at a similar abstention level. We claim no theoretical link between alpha and the RETAKE rate: alpha controls conformal miscoverage, and the abstention a conformal method induces is an emergent property of its prediction sets, not a controlled quantity. No tuned quantity in this paper uses the test split. Because Mondrian at the 0.90 level abstains on about 10% of test cases, the comparison at this target is approximately matched in abstention; Section 4.3 also reports the abstention rules at targets 0.05 and 0.15 so that the conclusion does not rest on one target. As a calibrated point baseline for the risk-coverage view we also fit isotonic regression on the calibration split and sweep its threshold. For the set-valued methods, a confident singleton violation maps to REJECT, a confident compliant to ACCEPT, and an ambiguous or empty set to RETAKE; for the abstention rules, abstention maps to RETAKE."));

// ---------------- 4 results ----------------
children.push(h1("4. Results"));

children.push(h2("4.1 The guarantee is feature-agnostic; the accuracy is a dataset artifact"));
children.push(P("Because TONO is single-violation, every per-criterion classifier can exploit the other 13 BioGaze decisions: when another criterion is flagged, this criterion is almost certainly compliant. Table 1 quantifies this. With the full feature set the logistic layer removes 66% of BioGaze's false flags; with only this criterion's own decision plus the continuous metrics the reduction falls to 26%; and with the continuous metrics alone the false-positive rate rises above the raw analyzer. Yet the conformal coverage is essentially unchanged across all three feature sets, exactly as the distribution-free theory predicts. The reading, shown in Figure 2, is that validity is insensitive to the feature set while accuracy is not: with weak features Mondrian still reaches 0.93 violation coverage, but only by inflating its prediction sets (mean size 1.55, with 55% of sets containing both classes), which is uninformative for triage. The accuracy gains are therefore tied to the single-violation structure and should not be expected to carry over to real multi-violation data. The coverage property, by contrast, is robust to feature quality within the evaluated distribution; whether it holds on real multi-attribute data is an empirical question that remains to be tested (Section 6)."));
children.push(new Paragraph({ keepNext: true, children: [new TextRun({ text: "Table 1. ", bold: true }), new TextRun("Feature ablation (five subject-disjoint seeds). The coverage guarantee is stable across feature sets; the logistic accuracy is not, and weak-feature coverage is held only by ambiguous sets.")], spacing: { after: 80 } }));
