children.push(h2("4.3 The false-flag versus detection trade-off"));
children.push(P("Table 3 compares the seven strategies on the same five subject-disjoint partitions. Three observations are essential. First, most of the false-flag reduction comes from recalibration, not conformal: a plain logistic layer already lowers the false-positive rate from 0.144 to 0.048 while keeping recall at 0.94. Second, the conformal methods do not win on accuracy. When plotted as a risk-coverage curve (Figure 4) the conformal operating points lie on or slightly below the isotonic-calibrated baseline, that is, they are matched or weakly dominated by a calibrated classifier with a swept threshold. Third, once abstention is allowed, the calibration-tuned abstention rules reach lower false-positive rates than Mondrian at a similar or lower RETAKE rate (Chow 0.027 at 9.5% RETAKE, the asymmetric rule 0.013 at 7.3%, Mondrian 0.040 at 10.3%), while recall among decided cases is comparable across all abstaining methods (0.94 to 0.96). Randomized APS is competitive (false-positive rate 0.026 at recall 0.89 over all cases); the non-randomized APS variant degenerates to trivial two-class sets on this binary problem, a property of that variant, not of APS. Because an abstaining method can improve both FPR and recall simply by routing hard cases to RETAKE, Table 3 reports recall both over all violations and among decided cases, together with the RETAKE rate and the selective error. The recall in Table 3 is measured on the common test partitions and is a distinct quantity from the per-criterion violation coverage of Table 2. Table 4 repeats the abstention rules at targets 0.05 and 0.15: the ranking does not depend on the target, and 0.10 is in fact the most favourable matched point available to Mondrian, whose abstention over the whole alpha sweep is never below about 0.10."));
children.push(new Paragraph({ keepNext: true, children: [new TextRun({ text: "Table 3. ", bold: true }), new TextRun("Consolidated comparison of seven triage strategies on one consistent protocol (mean plus or minus SD over the same five subject-disjoint partitions). Recall (all) counts an abstained violation as missed; recall (dec.) is measured among decided cases only. Decision coverage is 1 minus the RETAKE rate; selective error is the error rate among decided cases. FPR is computed over all compliant cases, abstained ones included; Figure 5 uses decided cases only. All thresholds are tuned on calibration only (Section 3.4). No single method dominates: the asymmetric rule attains the lowest FPR and selective error, Mondrian and APS the highest recall over all cases among abstaining methods, and only the conformal methods carry a finite-sample coverage guarantee.")], spacing: { after: 80 } }));
