children.push(new Paragraph({ text: "", spacing: { after: 100 } }));
children.push(P("Figure 5 traces each abstaining method across operating points, plotting the false-positive rate among decided cases against the abstention (RETAKE) rate. The conformal methods are plotted as points over a sweep of alpha, because their abstention rate is not monotone in alpha: it falls as alpha decreases and empty sets disappear, then rises again as sets become ambiguous. At any given abstention rate the calibration-tuned abstention rules reach a false-positive rate lower than or equal to that of either conformal method, and no method dominates once the guarantee is left out of the comparison. A conventional abstention rule therefore provides an equal or better empirical operating point. What it does not provide is a controllable class-conditional guarantee. On a binary criterion the conformal methods are themselves threshold rules: marginal LAC is, criterion by criterion, Chow's rule with tau equal to max(q, 1 minus q) for the single calibration quantile q (pooled over 14 criteria the LAC points in Figure 5 differ from the Chow curve only because LAC sets q per criterion whereas the Chow sweep uses one common tau), and Mondrian is a class-conditional two-threshold rule whose thresholds are the per-class calibration quantiles of the nonconformity score. Table 3 therefore compares two rule families, a symmetric confidence band and an asymmetric two-threshold band, each instantiated twice: with thresholds fixed as quantiles at the nominal level, or searched against an abstention or cost objective. The quantile choice is what gives Mondrian its property: its violation-class coverage is set directly by the nominal level 1 minus alpha and holds in finite samples under exchangeability, with no threshold searched against a cost objective. Figure 5 (right) shows the achieved violation coverage following the nominal target across the sweep; it sits 0.003 to 0.011 below nominal at the four levels from 0.70 to 0.90 and slightly above at tighter levels, a shortfall of the size that residual within-subject dependence or a calibration-to-test subject shift would produce, and consistent with the exchangeability caveat of Section 4.7. The abstention rules' violation coverage is instead an uncontrolled by-product of a tuned band, and marginal LAC's is systematically below target (0.82 at the 0.90 level). The contribution of Mondrian in this setting is therefore a principled recipe for choosing the two thresholds, carrying a finite-sample validity property that a tuned rule does not have, together with the demonstration that the marginal quantile fails on the minority class; it is not a win on FPR or recall."));
children.push(...figure(R3 + "fig_riskcoverage.png", 390, 300, "Figure 4. Risk-coverage view (partition 1). Conformal operating points lie on or slightly below the isotonic-calibrated baseline, so conformal is matched or weakly dominated by a calibrated classifier on the accuracy trade-off; its value is the guarantee, not the curve."));
children.push(...figure(R3 + "fig_risk_abstention.png", 500, 226, "Figure 5. Left: risk-abstention operating points (five partitions pooled), false-positive rate among decided cases versus RETAKE rate; the calibration-tuned abstention rules (lines) reach an FPR lower than or equal to the conformal methods (points, alpha sweep) at any abstention rate. Right: Mondrian's achieved violation-class coverage tracks the nominal target 1 minus alpha, the property the abstention rules do not have."));

children.push(new Paragraph({ text: "", spacing: { after: 60 } }));
children.push(h2("4.4 Exploratory image-level triage on TONO"));
children.push(P("Aggregating the Mondrian per-criterion sets into an image decision yields 0 ACCEPT, 958 REJECT and 36 RETAKE on the 994-image test set of the first partition (Figure 6). This set contains 925 in-scope violation images and 69 out-of-scope zoom images (Section 3.2). A correct ACCEPT is structurally unreachable on the in-scope images, since each violates exactly one criterion by construction. The only available proxy for image-level specificity is the 69 zoom images, which are compliant on all 14 checked criteria; all 69 were rejected (0 of 69 accepted). Zoom images are out of distribution for the head-size metric and were excluded from training and calibration, so this proxy is weak, but it is the only one TONO offers and it did not pass. Because a naive REJECT count conflates rejecting for the right reason with rejecting for the wrong one, we decompose the outcome on the 925 in-scope violation images by whether the truly-violated criterion is the one flagged. We find 822 correct-target REJECTs (88.9%, the real violation is confidently flagged), 67 wrong-reason REJECTs (7.2%, the image is rejected but on a different criterion, so the real violation is missed even though the photo is still stopped), 36 RETAKEs (3.9%), and 0 ACCEPTs. The triage flags the correct criterion in the large majority of cases; the 7.2% wrong-reason rejections are the failure mode to report, and the empty ACCEPT class means the zero count of accepted violations carries no information about safety. We do not over-claim a general safety property: TONO is single-violation, so essentially no genuinely compliant images exist, the ACCEPT class is empty, and image-level specificity (the risk of wrongly rejecting a compliant photo) cannot be tested here. A real multi-attribute dataset with a genuine, in-distribution ACCEPT class is needed to evaluate this."));
children.push(...figure(R3 + "fig_imgdec_v3.png", 470, 320, "Figure 6. Image-level triage under Mondrian (partition 1), with REJECT decomposed into correct-target, wrong-reason and out-of-scope zoom images. No in-scope violation image is wrongly accepted; ACCEPT is empty because TONO contains no genuinely compliant in-scope images."));
children.push(h2("4.5 Joint coverage and the multiple-comparison problem"));
children.push(P("The per-criterion guarantees are marginal in the criteria. Requiring all 14 prediction sets to contain the truth simultaneously gives only about 0.27 empirical joint coverage, the expected multiple-comparison effect. A Bonferroni correction (per-criterion target 0.993) raises empirical joint coverage to about 0.91 for both marginal and Mondrian conformal, but the price is decisiveness. For marginal LAC about 96% of images are routed to RETAKE. For Mondrian the corrected violation-class threshold is vacuous at this calibration size: a level of 0.993 needs at least 139 calibration positives per criterion and the largest criterion has 117, so every set contains the violation class and every image is routed to RETAKE (Figure 7); its 0.91 joint coverage comes entirely from the finite compliant-class thresholds. The marginal method keeps a finite threshold only because it pools both classes (about 850 calibration images), and it attains 0.91 joint coverage by routing 96% of images to RETAKE. A Bonferroni image-level guarantee is therefore attainable but not useful at this scale; a tighter simultaneous guarantee is future work."));
children.push(...figure(R3 + "fig_joint_v3.png", 460, 290, "Figure 7. Naive joint coverage over 14 criteria collapses to about 0.27; a Bonferroni correction raises it to about 0.91 empirically, but routes 96% (LAC) or all (Mondrian, whose corrected violation threshold is infinite at this calibration size) of the images to RETAKE."));
children.push(h2("4.6 Robustness"));
children.push(P("The conclusions are stable across the checks we ran. Marginal overall coverage tracks the nominal target across levels (targets 0.95, 0.90 and 0.80 give empirical 0.951, 0.899 and 0.803), and the Mondrian improvement in violation coverage holds at every level of the alpha sweep in Figure 5 and on each of the five partitions individually (Mondrian violation coverage 0.88 to 0.91 versus marginal 0.80 to 0.84). Randomized APS gives the same picture across partitions and across the two independent randomization streams of our two analysis scripts (recall 0.889 and 0.891, set size about 1.07). We did not vary the split ratio."));
children.push(h2("4.7 Exchangeability and cluster-aware sensitivity"));
children.push(P("Split conformal prediction requires exchangeability of the calibration and test scores. Subject-disjoint splitting removes identity leakage, but it does not by itself establish image-level exchangeability, because a subject contributes several correlated images. We take one position throughout the paper: the guarantee is claimed at the image level under the exchangeability assumption, subject-disjoint splitting is presented as protection against identity leakage rather than as proof of exchangeability, and the sensitivity of the result to within-subject dependence is tested empirically. The structure is as follows. Every partition holds 165 training, 55 calibration and 55 test subjects; the calibration split holds 760 to 1020 images (median 14 to 15 per subject) and the test split 780 to 1080 images (median 14 to 15 per subject). The single largest subject contributes about 8% of the calibration set on average (at most 14%) and about 10% of the test set (at most 16%); no subject exceeds 16% of either split."));
children.push(P("To test sensitivity to within-subject dependence we separate the effect of clustering from the effect of sample size. Capping each subject to at most three calibration images gives a calibration set of at most 165 images (three per calibration subject) and Mondrian violation coverage 0.94 (SD 0.02 over partitions); a random image subsample of exactly the same size, ignoring subjects, gives 0.94 (SD 0.02). The two agree, so at equal n we could not detect any clustering effect: the shift from 0.90 to 0.94 is the conservatism of the exact quantile at small n, not a subject effect. A subject-level bootstrap of the full calibration set (resampling subjects with replacement, keeping multiplicity) gives mean 0.89 with a 5th to 95th percentile range of 0.86 to 0.91, which contains the 0.90 target; the standard deviation of coverage across test subjects is about 0.12, which quantifies the heterogeneity that a subject-level guarantee would have to absorb (Figure 8). This check has limited power, because the capped and the random subsamples contain about three images per subject either way, so the contrast in clustering between the two arms is small by construction. A more direct diagnostic is the spread of Mondrian violation coverage across the five partitions: its standard deviation is 0.010, essentially the binomial value expected under independence at about 850 violation evaluations per partition (0.010); a substantial design effect from within-subject dependence would appear as excess between-partition variance and does not. Within the power of these checks, the main result is not an artifact of within-subject dependence. A formal clustered or subject-level conformal guarantee remains future work."));
children.push(...figure(R3 + "fig_subject_sensitivity.png", 430, 256, "Figure 8. Cluster-aware sensitivity of Mondrian violation-class coverage. Subject-capped and random equal-n subsamples agree, so no clustering effect is detected beyond the small-n conservatism of the exact quantile; the subject bootstrap range contains the 0.90 target."));

// ---------------- 5 explainability ----------------
children.push(h1("5. Auditability"));
children.push(P("Because the underlying analyzer is rule-based, each BioGaze decision can be accompanied by a region-level explanation from face-parsing segmentation and 68-point landmarks that localises the evidence the rule used (for example the lens region for sunglasses or the eyelid geometry for closed eyes). These explanations are faithful to BioGaze's own rule, not to the meta-classifier's decision: Section 4.1 shows that on TONO the meta-classifier draws substantially on cross-criterion features, which a region map does not explain. We therefore present Figure 9 as a demonstration of what can be shown to an applicant alongside a REJECT or RETAKE, not as evidence of an audited pipeline."));
children.push(...figure(R1 + "xai/xai_panel.png", 225, 537, "Figure 9. Region-level explanations of BioGaze's rule-based decisions on TONO examples (demonstration)."));

// ---------------- 6 limitations ----------------
children.push(new Paragraph({ text: "", spacing: { after: 60 } }));
children.push(h1("6. Discussion"));
children.push(P2("Which method, for which pipeline. ", "The results support a simple operational reading. If the goal is only to reduce false flags, a logistic recalibration of the analyzer's outputs achieves most of it with no abstention machinery at all. If a fixed retake budget and the best empirical operating point are what matter, a calibration-tuned abstention rule, especially the asymmetric two-threshold rule under a miss-weighted cost, is the strongest choice in our experiments: it reaches a lower false-positive rate than any conformal method at an equal or lower RETAKE rate. Mondrian conformal earns its place when the binding requirement is a documented, auditable statement about the miss rate on true violations: its violation-class coverage level is fixed in advance as a policy parameter, holds in finite samples under exchangeability, and requires no threshold search that an auditor would have to re-validate. The price, one to three points of false-positive rate at a similar retake rate, is the measurable cost of that formal property, and whether it is worth paying is a decision for the issuing authority, not for this paper."));
children.push(P2("What alpha does and does not control. ", "The nominal level 1 minus alpha controls the miscoverage of the violation class, and nothing else. In particular it does not control the RETAKE rate, which is an emergent property of the prediction sets: in our experiments Mondrian's abstention is never below about 10% at any alpha, and it rises at both extremes of the sweep. A deployment that needs a hard cap on retakes therefore cannot obtain it by tuning alpha, and should either adopt a tuned abstention rule or combine the conformal layer with an explicit budget mechanism. Conversely, a deployment that must certify a miss rate cannot obtain that from a tuned rule, whose violation coverage is an uncontrolled by-product of its thresholds."));
children.push(P2("Internal robustness versus external validity. ", "Within the evaluated distribution the coverage property behaved as the theory predicts: it was insensitive to feature quality in the ablation, stable across ten repeated partitions, and not measurably affected by within-subject dependence in the cluster diagnostics. None of this establishes validity beyond TONO. The dataset is synthetic, single-violation by construction, and contains no genuine in-scope compliant images, so the ACCEPT decision, image-level specificity, and behaviour under co-occurring violations are all untested. We therefore state the claim narrowly: the coverage property is robust to feature quality within the evaluated distribution, and its validity on real, multi-attribute data remains to be tested."));
children.push(P2("External validation on DFIC. ", "The natural next step is the DFIC dataset: real photographs of more than 1,000 subjects, annotated for 26 requirements, with genuinely compliant portraits and a demographic balance that TONO lacks. Running the identical pipeline on DFIC would test the four claims of this paper where they can fail: whether marginal conformal still under-covers the violation class on real data, whether Mondrian still brings it close to target with usable set sizes, whether the accuracy artifact identified in Section 4.1 disappears as predicted once violations co-occur, and, for the first time, whether the image-level ACCEPT decision can be made with controlled specificity. It would also allow a cost-calibrated RETAKE region to be tuned against realistic miss and retake costs. We regard this external validation as the decisive test of the paper's central claim and plan it as the immediate continuation of this work."));
children.push(h2("6.1 Limitations"));
children.push(bullet("Recalibration versus conformal. Most of the false-flag reduction is achieved by recalibration alone; conformal's added value is the distribution-free guarantee and, through Mondrian, the class-conditional guarantee, not a further accuracy gain."));
children.push(bullet("Single-violation artifact. TONO's structure lets the classifier exploit cross-criterion signals, so the accuracy numbers are upper bounds tied to the dataset and are not expected to carry over to multi-violation data. The coverage property is robust to feature quality within the evaluated distribution; its validity on real data is untested."));
children.push(bullet("Empty ACCEPT class. TONO has no genuinely compliant in-scope images, so image-level specificity and the ACCEPT decision cannot be validated in-distribution here; the only proxy, 69 out-of-scope zoom images, was rejected in full."));
children.push(bullet("Subject-level guarantee. We provide an empirical subject-level diagnostic, not a formal clustered-conformal guarantee; the check can fail to detect a clustering effect smaller than its sampling noise."));
children.push(bullet("Validity versus usefulness. Mondrian's advantage is a validity property, not an accuracy or false-flag advantage: a calibration-tuned abstention rule reaches a lower false-positive rate at the same retake rate. Whether the formal guarantee is worth that cost is an operational decision that this study informs but does not settle."));
children.push(bullet("Confidence intervals. The primary intervals of Table 2 come from a subject-cluster bootstrap on one fixed partition, which respects within-subject dependence by construction but conditions on that partition; partition-to-partition variability is addressed separately by the ten-partition robustness summary. With about 55 test subjects per partition the intervals are wide, which is the honest price of the clustered design."));
children.push(bullet("Joint coverage. Per-criterion guarantees do not compose into an image-level guarantee without a conservative multiple-comparison correction."));
children.push(bullet("Small-sample criteria. Coverage estimates for rare criteria (for example Frontal pose) are unstable and are flagged rather than relied upon."));
children.push(P("Section 6 sets out how the external validation on DFIC addresses the dataset-bound limitations above; the methodological ones, a formal clustered guarantee and a non-vacuous simultaneous guarantee, remain open problems."));

// ---------------- 7 conclusion ----------------
children.push(h1("7. Conclusion"));
children.push(P("We showed that a rule-based ICAO compliance analyzer can be wrapped with a per-criterion conformal layer that supplies a distribution-free coverage guarantee under image-level exchangeability, evaluated under a strictly subject-disjoint protocol. The central empirical finding is the size of the marginal method's failure on the minority violation class, down to 0.40 on a criterion whose overall coverage is on target, while label-conditional Mondrian conformal brings violation-class coverage close to the nominal target on every stable criterion, consistent with label-conditional validity. A feature ablation separates the coverage property, robust to feature quality within the evaluated distribution, from the single-violation accuracy artifact, and a seven-method comparison with calibration-only tuning shows that recalibration provides most of the accuracy, that a conventional abstention rule can provide an equal or better empirical operating point, and that Mondrian's distinct contribution is the formal class-conditional coverage property. We characterise the multiple-comparison problem of a 14-criterion decision and confine every claim to what the dataset can support. The immediate next step is external validation on the real, multi-attribute DFIC dataset, where the ACCEPT decision and behaviour under co-occurring violations can be tested for the first time, together with a cost-calibrated and simultaneously valid triage."));

